Ohle Claussen <jann.ohle.claussen@stud.uni-goettingen.de> writes:
> First of all: I don't have any idea whether this has ever been
> suggested or how well implementable it is.
> 
> I think it would add physical consistency if corpses of monsters remain
> warm and therefor infravisible for, say, 10 turns after the monster has
> been killed. The age of a corpse is tracked anyway, so that eases things
> up a little. This would make sense, and it would make it possible to
> determine whether a given monster that you killed via ranged attacks in
> a dark corridor has left a corpse. 
> 
> Of course, this should not apply to corpses of monsters that were not
> infravisible to start with.

Without touching the question of whether "physical consistency" is
desirable or achievable:

Adding infravisible objects is potentially possible, but a pain. I
wouldn't want to try adding them without bundling the addition
together with invisible objects (for which defined-out code-in-progress
exists in the source, but I'm not sure at what stage of completion;
presumably it's borrowed to some extent from SLASH'EM's code for the
same concept, though I have no idea how far the similarities go).

The current assumptions are a) that if you can see (or remember) any
objects on a square, you can see (or remember) all objects on that
square; and b) that the topmost object you can see or remember is
always the one that should be mapped. Neither of these conditions hold
under your proposal, so would need to be changed; INVISIBLE_OBJECTS
makes those changes in its own way, and I wouldn't want to clash with
that.

(I'll note that if you _do_ add infravisible objects, there are other
ones you'll want to consider; recently-extinguished lamps and candles,
for example.)

